the rallies across russia have been  banned but that didn't stop people and  kaburovsk from coming out  to make their voices heard the eastern  city has seen months of protests against  the kremlin  but this rally was specifically in  support of president vladimir putin's  fiercest critic alexei navalny  there were soon scuffles with police  authorities have gone to great lengths  to prevent  such acts of defiance including  arresting several of navalny's top aides  in the lead-up to saturday's protests  the kremlins described the rallies as  unlawful and warned of tough  consequences  calls for participation in illegal mass  actions constitute an administrative  violation  and can lead to punishment including  arrest  it's alexi navalny's arrest last week  that's behind the protests  he was taken into custody soon after he  touched down in moscow  on a flight from germany where he'd  received months of treatment for a  poisoning which nearly killed him  he's accused president putin of ordering  his murder  authorities say navalny violated the  terms of a 2014 conviction for financial  misdeeds  and jailed him for 30 days in a video  posted soon after his arrest he said  don't be silent resist take to the  streets no one will protect us  except ourselves his supporters have  answered his calls  dozens of videos on tech talk have gone  viral showing students taking down  pictures of putin in their classrooms  or covering his photo with navalny's  image instead  the kremlins warned online platforms  they could face  fines of up to fifty thousand dollars  for allowing content that  encourages protests russia's largest  social media network v  contact has blocked all pages linked to  the rallies  the russian investigative committee has  initiated a criminal case on enticement  of minors into illegal activities  that pose a danger to their lives  carried out via internet and social  media  mass protests have led to navalny's  release from custody before  but some analysts believe the tactic  won't work this time  because he's become too much of a threat  to president putin's grip  on power alex o'brien al jazeera
 

